<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gijón</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xixón</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gijón is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> with Roman origins. In the eighth century it was a seat of Muslim authority whose governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munuza">Munuza</a> faced the revolt of Pelagius. It grew into an industrial and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-exporting port, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> it was the seat of the Sovereign Council of Asturias and León until Franco's forces took it on 21 October 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gijon.jpg" alt="Gijón" width="320" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Veriña · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xix%C3%B3n_dende_l%27aire_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gijón lies on the coast of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, about 24 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> and 26 km from Avilés. The peninsula of Cimavilla, the original settlement, separates the beach of San Lorenzo to the east from the beaches of Poniente and Arbeyal, the shipyards, the recreational port and the Port of El Musel to the west. The ground rises from sea level to 513 m at Picu Samartín and 672 m at Peña de los Cuatro Jueces. The name may come from Gigia, a medieval form tied to the Roman wall on the Cimavilla peninsula, called the Gegionem, or from a hypothetical Roman name Sessio that became Xixón; the second theory is now thought the more acceptable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest evidence of human presence is a series of tumuli on Monte Deva and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> dolmens on Monte Areo, supposedly built around 5000 BCE. The first settlement, Noega at Campa Torres, began between the 6th and 5th centuries BCE, was populated by Astures and later Romanized, and was gradually abandoned once the Roman wall on Cimavilla was built. The region submitted to the Visigoth king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisebut">Sisebut</a> in the 7th century. Gijón was the capital of the Muslim territories on the Cantabrian Sea under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munuza">Munuza</a> for a short period between 713 and 718 or 722. In 722 the Asturians led by Pelagius, the first king of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-covadonga">Battle of Covadonga</a>, regarded as the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>.</p><p>Until 1270 there are no reliable references to Gijón as a settlement. That year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a> gave it the status of puebla. In the 14th century the war between Alfonso Enríquez, Count of Gijón and Noreña, and Henry III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> ended with the village burned and destroyed. It re-emerged in the 15th and 16th centuries, when a new dock added fishing and commerce, and in the 17th and 18th centuries it developed rapidly through the commercial links of its port with the American colonies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>–Gijón road and the recognition of the port as the best in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> started industrial activity late in the 18th century.</p><p>The 19th century brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> trade, the Gijón–León road and the Langreo–Gijón railway, and in 1893 a new port, El Musel, was built as the first coal port of the peninsula. New neighbourhoods such as Natahoyo, La Calzada, Tremañes and El Humedal housed the workers the industry drew. Iron manufacture was the main industry from the last years of the 19th century until the last decades of the 20th. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> the city supported the Republican side, and the resistance was eliminated in August 1936. It was then the capital of the Sovereign Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> and León until General Franco's troops occupied it in October 1937.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gijón” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the Asturian chronicles of the late ninth century, Pelagius, or Pelayo, a Visigothic noble who may have served in the royal guard, was chosen as leader by the Asturians and led a revolt against the Muslim governor Munuza at <b>Gijón</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 24 August 1937 the Interprovincial Council at <b>Gijón</b>, led by the Socialist miners' leader Belarmino Tomás, declared itself sovereign.</blockquote><a href="/state/sovereign-council-of-asturias-and-leon">Sovereign Council of Asturias and León</a></li></ul></section>
